    "id": "builder_blog:builder_blog:https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/26/sqlite3-rsync/",
    "source": "builder_blog",
    "sourceItemId": "builder_blog:https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/26/sqlite3-rsync/",
    "title": "sqlite3_rsync: Database Remote-Copy Tool For SQLite",
    "summary": "<p><strong><a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/rsync.html\">sqlite3_rsync: Database Remote-Copy Tool For SQLite</a></strong></p> This additional SQLite tool has been around for a while now - it was added as \"experimental\" <a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/releaselog/3_47_0.html\">in SQLite 3.47.0 in October 2024</a> and enhanced in <a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/releaselog/3_50_0.html\">SQLite 3.50.0 in May 2025</a>. It was only today that I understood one of its most attractive characteristics.</p> <p>",
    "body": "<p><strong><a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/rsync.html\">sqlite3_rsync: Database Remote-Copy Tool For SQLite</a></strong></p> This additional SQLite tool has been around for a while now - it was added as \"experimental\" <a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/releaselog/3_47_0.html\">in SQLite 3.47.0 in October 2024</a> and enhanced in <a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/releaselog/3_50_0.html\">SQLite 3.50.0 in May 2025</a>. It was only today that I understood one of its most attractive characteristics.</p> <p>Normally, if you want to grab a snapshot copy of a SQLite database, you need to use either the <a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/backup.html\">backup API</a> or <a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_vacuum.html#vacuuminto\">VACUUM INTO</a>. In both cases you need enough disk space to create a duplicate c",
    "url": "https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/26/sqlite3-rsync/",
